<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aphrodite</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cytherea, Cypris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aphrodite is the ancient Greek goddess associated with love and beauty, whose main cult centres were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Cythera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Corinth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> she is the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dione">Dione</a>, while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a> she is born from the sea foam produced by the severed genitals of Uranus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> she is married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a> and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> as her lover, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> she was one of the three goddesses whose feud led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>. Her origins are debated: proposed influences include Near Eastern love goddesses, an indigenous Cypriot goddess and a Proto-Indo-European dawn goddess. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified her with their goddess Venus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aphrodite.jpg" alt="Aphrodite" width="330" height="772" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Praxiteles; restorer: Ippolito Buzzi (Italian, 1562–1634) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cnidus_Aphrodite_Altemps_Inv8619.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> derives the name Aphrodite from aphrós, sea-foam, but most modern scholars regard this as a folk etymology. The name is generally accepted as probably of Semitic origin, though its exact derivation cannot be determined with confidence. Fritz Hommel suggested a hellenized pronunciation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>, a view others reject as linguistically untenable. Martin West reconstructs a Cyprian Canaanite form and cautiously suggests an epithet meaning She of the Villages. Hesiod's account of her birth is probably derived from the Hittite Song of Kumarbi, in which Kumarbi bites off the genitals of his father Anu, and Ishtar is among the children that result.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Her most common cultic epithet was Ourania, heavenly, and another was Pandemos, for all the folk. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Symposium the character Pausanias takes the two epithets to name separate goddesses. Her main festival, the Aphrodisia, was held across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Corinth. In Athens it fell on the fourth day of the month of Hekatombaion; priests purified the temple of Aphrodite Pandemos on the southwestern slope of the Acropolis with the blood of a sacrificed dove, and the statues of Aphrodite and Peitho were carried in procession to be ritually bathed. The fourth day of every month was sacred to her.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified her with the Egyptian goddesses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>. She was the patron goddess of the Ptolemaic queens, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a> was identified as her mortal incarnation and introduced the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In the second century BCE Ptolemy VIII Physcon and his wives dedicated a temple to Aphrodite Hathor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>. Her myths include the judgment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in which Paris chose her over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> after she offered him the hand of Helen. In the First Homeric Hymn she is made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> to fall in love with the mortal Anchises. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> she helps adorn Pandora.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The dove was her most prominent bird, a symbol inherited from her Near Eastern precursor Inanna-Ishtar, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> describes her riding in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> pulled by sparrows. The Ludovisi Throne, of about 460 BCE, shows her rising from the sea. Early Christians adapted her iconography to Eve and to female saints, and in the late fifth century Fulgentius of Ruspe read her mosaics as a symbol of lust. Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painters took up her birth from the foam, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandro-botticelli">Sandro Botticelli</a> in The Birth of Venus of about 1485. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>'s poem Venus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a> (1593) was the most popular of his works published in his lifetime.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aphrodite” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greek cults of Apollo, <b>Aphrodite</b> and Demeter coexisted with local deities, and in the Roman period Panticapaeum and other towns had Jewish communities whose inscriptions record the freeing of slaves in the synagogue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…were shaped by the older peoples of the Aegean and the civilisations of the Near East: Hesiod’s myth of the succession of the gods resembles Hurrian and Hittite myths of the kingship in heaven, and <b>Aphrodite</b> has much in common with the Near Eastern goddess Ishtar and Astarte.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greeks matched these gods with their own: Melqart with Heracles, Astarte with <b>Aphrodite</b>, Eshmun with Asclepius.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Most honoured was Tabiti, whom he equates with Hestia, goddess of the hearth; then Papaeus, equated with Zeus, and Api, the Earth, his wife; then gods he calls Apollo, <b>Aphrodite</b> Urania, Heracles and Ares, with Poseidon worshipped by the Royal Scythians.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>A two-word stock epithet can fill half a line, freeing the poet to compose the rest, and small inconsistencies, such as <b>Aphrodite</b> remaining “laughter-loving” after Diomedes has painfully wounded her, may be effects of that tradition.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Arsinoe II was deified, with her own sanctuaries and festivals, and shown both in Greek forms, as <b>Aphrodite</b> or Hera, and as an Egyptian goddess; Berenice II was queen to Ptolemy III.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
